\DOC SIMPLE_ARITH_TAC

\TYPE {SIMPLE_ARITH_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
{SIMPLE_ARITH_TAC} proves the goal's conclusion by linear arithmetic from the
goal's own assumptions, closing it outright on success.

\DESCRIBE
It takes no arguments. Applied to {A ?- t}, it either closes the goal or fails;
the observed traces show no case where it transforms the goal or leaves
subgoals.
{
           A ?- t
   =============== SIMPLE_ARITH_TAC
}
\noindent It uses the assumptions in {A} as well as the conclusion: {t} may be
a plain numeric inequality or equation, a conjunction or disjunction of such, a
universally quantified or conditional statement, or an equation between
word/address terms, and is proved from arithmetic facts present among the
assumptions. Irrelevant assumptions (register reads, memory reads, {MAYCHANGE}
relations, nonoverlapping conditions, large event traces) are tolerated and
left untouched. Successful traces include both goals with no assumptions and
goals with large assumption lists.

\FAILURE
Fails with an arithmetic-decision-procedure exception reporting that no
contradiction could be derived, when the conclusion is not a consequence of the
assumptions by linear arithmetic over the naturals/integers: for instance when
it is simply false as stated, when the needed facts are nonlinear or involve
congruences, divisibility or other non-arithmetic predicates that the procedure
abstracts away, or when the conclusion contains terms (such as memory/word
reads) treated as opaque atoms so that no arithmetic reasoning can establish
it. The failure comes from the underlying arithmetic decision procedure invoked
by the tactic.

\ENDDOC
